1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.035
After
you develop your agent and improve it

2
00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:05.105
until it's production ready,
you need to apply the same techniques

3
00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:08.675
of tracing evals and experimentation
to your agent in production.

4
00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:10.210
During production,

5
00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.312
things like code changes or model updates

6
00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.280
can degrade the performance of your agent.

7
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.951
You can use your evals and run experiments
to continuously monitor

8
00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:20.787
and improve your agent. Let's get to it.

9
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.758
As a quick recap, you've covered
four main steps that take you from

10
00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:28.495
an initial agent prototype all the way
through to a production-ready system.

11
00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:30.997
Choosing the right architecture,

12
00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:33.967
you need to decide on an agent framework
that matches your use case.

13
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.202
Deciding which eval to use.

14
00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.972
Figuring out which metrics really matter
for your system,

15
00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:41.975
such as accuracy, latency,
and convergence.

16
00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:45.645
Building your evaluation structure,
which includes the prompts,

17
00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:48.815
tools, and data
you'll use to measure performance.

18
00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:52.752
Iterating with your data,
you keep refining your agent by

19
00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:56.556
analyzing results, adjusting prompts
or logic, and testing again.

20
00:00:57.223 --> 00:01:01.294
This cycle repeats as you move
from development to production and back

21
00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:05.832
again, so you can catch issues early
and refine based on real-world feedback.

22
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:10.270
When you actually reach production,
you may find that the simple agent designs

23
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.273
you use
initially need to scale up and complexity.

24
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.843
You might end up
adopting a multi-agent system

25
00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:19.779
where agents can call
other specialized agents,

26
00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:23.316
or a multimodal system where your agent
handles different types of data,

27
00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:28.354
like text or images or audio,
or a continuous improving system

28
00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:31.658
where your agent learns
from user interactions in real time,

29
00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:35.061
either through manual updates
or automated processes.

30
00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:38.665
So, what's different about production?

31
00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:41.668
Well, you can often discover new failure
modes.

32
00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.771
Users might ask you questions
that you never saw in development

33
00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:47.574
or they reference something
that your system doesn't know about yet,

34
00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:49.309
like a brand new product.

35
00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:53.046
If your agent now calls out
to additional APIs or other agents,

36
00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:56.449
there are more chances for errors
or unexpected outputs.

37
00:01:56.783 --> 00:02:00.353
You might also try A/B testing
or different model strategies

38
00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:02.789
that introduce surprising regressions

39
00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:04.991
that you didn't anticipate
in a controlled environment.

40
00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:06.860
The encouraging

41
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.863
part here is that a lot of the tools
you use in development,

42
00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:14.234
like instrumentation and feedback
loops, are just as valuable in production.

43
00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:17.904
You'll continue to collect metrics
that you define for evaluation,

44
00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:22.142
and you'll rely on continuous integration
and continuous delivery flows

45
00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:25.812
and ongoing experiments
to keep tabs on your agent's performance.

46
00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:29.916
In other words, you can use those
same tracing and annotation methods

47
00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:33.520
you had in development, only
now, they're enriched by real user data.

48
00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:37.023
You'll gather
feedback from actual interactions, label

49
00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:40.760
any problematic outputs, and identify
where your agent might be struggling.

50
00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:45.198
Collecting user feedback
for evals is crucial in production.

51
00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:49.802
You can take real usage data, for example,
every user query or interaction,

52
00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:54.073
and attach human-labeled annotations
to highlight issues or successes.

53
00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:58.711
If your eval metrics disagree
with what real users say, that's a sign

54
00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:01.714
you might need to recheck your system
or your eval.

55
00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:03.516
Maybe you're measuring the wrong metric.

56
00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:06.286
Or maybe there's a deeper logic
flaw and the agent's flow.

57
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.322
You also want to keep track of metrics

58
00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:12.759
over time to understand efficiency
or execution dependencies.

59
00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:15.628
For example,
if you're using convergence evals,

60
00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:19.065
you can see how many steps it takes
for the agents to reach a correct answer.

61
00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:23.803
If that number grows after you tweak
your prompts, it can indicate a regression

62
00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:27.407
since you're likely calling external
LLM APIs,

63
00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:29.342
you should track those calls, too.

64
00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:30.944
They can have a significant impact

65
00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:34.247
on latency and costs,
which can impact your end user experience.

66
00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.049
Your choice of model,

67
00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:39.519
perhaps a large reasoning model versus
a smaller, faster one

68
00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:42.589
may vary based on the complexity of tasks
in production.

69
00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:46.226
Keeping an eye on those decisions
and their downstream

70
00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:49.229
effects is part of robust production
monitoring.

71
00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:53.633
When you gather human feedback
and run your evals on real traffic,

72
00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:57.503
you'll gain a clearer picture
of how changes like swapping out a model

73
00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:00.506
or updating a prompt, impact
the overall system.

74
00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:03.676
You can rerun the same experiments
you used in development,

75
00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:08.414
but now on new data or new failure modes
that you've discovered in production.

76
00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.651
This is why you'll want to maintain
consistent data

77
00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:14.654
sets and keep augmenting them
with production samples.

78
00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.857
One effective approach is curating golden
data sets that

79
00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:20.860
capture your most critical use cases
and known failure modes.

80
00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:24.297
Each time you push a change,
like adjusting a prompt or logic,

81
00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:28.201
you can recheck these data sets to be sure
you haven't broken something you've

82
00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:28.935
already solved.

83
00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.639
Experiments can act like gates
for shipping changes,

84
00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:35.742
so you can decide whether to roll forward
or roll back a change.

85
00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:39.979
Let's imagine you have a self-improving
agent that collects feedback

86
00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:42.982
automatically whenever a user interacts
with your system.

87
00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.852
You can add those user examples,
both successful

88
00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:49.255
and failed interactions,
to a continuously updated data set.

89
00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:53.026
Then you run
CI/CD experiments on this data set,

90
00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:56.195
checking
if new versions of the agent do better or

91
00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:59.198
worse on the very latest
real-world scenarios.

92
00:04:59.532 --> 00:05:02.535
When you refine the agent logic
or tweak prompts,

93
00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:06.339
you can also incorporate few shot examples
from the newly collected data.

94
00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:10.276
This lets your system learn from mistakes
and gradually converge

95
00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:13.980
towards better performance
on exactly the tasks that matter most

96
00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:17.216
in a self-improving and automated manner.

97
00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:20.553
What you're doing here
is essentially applying evaluation driven

98
00:05:20.553 --> 00:05:22.655
development in production.

99
00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.657
You're watching for new failure modes,

100
00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.960
monitoring how well your agent
handles them, and automating

101
00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:31.130
feeding production
feedback back into your evals.

102
00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:32.665
In this

103
00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:33.466
lesson, you've

104
00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:37.236
learned what to watch out for new queries,
complicated architectures,

105
00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:40.840
and unexpected user behavior,
and how to keep your agent on track

106
00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.843
by continuously measuring
and refining its performance.

107
00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:48.047
You've also seen how the tools and data
you used in development carry

108
00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:51.718
seamlessly over to production,
and how you might scale those approaches

109
00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.487
for even more robust agent systems,

110
00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:57.690
by using CI/CD pipelines
with consistent experiments,

111
00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:01.327
you can ensure that every update
to your agent maintains

112
00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:04.330
or improves on the quality
you've already achieved.

113
00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:08.534
Now, you have a solid foundation
for monitoring your agents in production

114
00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:12.205
and continuously refining their
performance based on real-world data.

